WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
When you're injecting multiple documents into a single prompt, the structure you choose determines

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.950
whether the model can keep them straight.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.600
Over the next few minutes, we'll cover how to inject multiple documents cleanly, when to use

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.504
sections versus separate turns, how to reference documents and instructions, and how to test that

5
00:00:18.504 --> 00:00:20.878
the model attributes outputs correctly.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Let's get into it.

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Picture a prompt carrying five separate source documents and a question that only applies to two of them.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Whether the model gets that right comes down almost entirely to structure.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.305
A multi-document prompt is any prompt that includes two or more source documents for the model to

10
00:00:40.305 --> 00:00:46.935
reason over, and each one needs its own labeled block so the model can reference and distinguish them.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.660
Document injection is the practice of placing source material into a prompt using structured tags,

12
00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:00.932
things like document, document content, and source, rather than pasting text inline into your instructions.

13
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.369
Output attribution is the model's ability to correctly link what it says to the document it came

14
00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:14.933
from, and reliable attribution depends almost entirely on how well your structure separates and labels the inputs.

15
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.410
When a task references multiple sources, each document needs its own consistently structured block,

16
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.935
and three tags cover almost every case.

17
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The document tag is the top-level container for a single source.

18
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
The document content tag holds the raw text and sits as a child inside it.

19
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.000
The source tag carries identifying metadata, a file name, a URL, or a short label, also as a child.

20
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.388
Numbering each document with an index attribute is a small addition that pays off when your

21
00:01:45.388 --> 00:01:48.940
instructions need to point at a specific source by position.

22
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.143
Keeping every block self-contained and parallel is what makes the structure legible to the model,

23
00:01:54.143 --> 00:01:55.946
and to you when you are debugging.

24
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
If one block carries a source and another doesn't, the model has to guess what that content is for.

25
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.488
Whether you put everything in one prompt or spread it across turns comes down to whether your task

26
00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:09.943
is parallel or sequential.

27
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.000
A single prompt with sections puts everything in one call, so the model sees all documents and instructions at once.

28
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
That is what you want when the task is to compare, contrast, or synthesize several sources together.

29
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
The tradeoff is length, and long prompts are harder to debug when something goes wrong.

30
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Separate conversational turns spread the task across messages, each one smaller and building on the prior response.

31
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.696
That suits sequential workflows, where you can catch an error and course-correct between steps

32
00:02:41.696 --> 00:02:43.939
instead of discovering it at the end.

33
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Neither is universally better. Let the task structure drive the choice.

34
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Explicit reference patterns inside your instructions are what connect the structure to the task.

35
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.000
You can reference by source name, asking the model to summarize the main argument in the document from source A.

36
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Or you can reference by index, asking it to use document 1 and document 2, and identify where they disagree.

37
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Both styles work, but explicit references help the model attribute its output to the right input.

38
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.387
Avoid vague phrases like the documents or the context when only one specific document should inform

39
00:03:21.387 --> 00:03:22.935
that part of the answer.

40
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Vague references are one of the main causes of output bleed, where a claim gets attributed to the wrong source.

41
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Three anti-patterns come up repeatedly in multi-document prompts.

42
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.295
The first is inline prose mixing, embedding document text directly inside instruction paragraphs

43
00:03:41.295 --> 00:03:42.934
without any tag boundary.

44
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:49.000
The model can't reliably separate what it should follow from what it should analyze.

45
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
The second is mixing data and directives in the same untagged block.

46
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.000
This is the core structure failure that enables prompt injection.

47
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.222
The third is sending a long, unformatted prompt with multiple documents and no tags at all, hoping

48
00:04:03.222 --> 00:04:04.936
the model finds everything.

49
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
As prompt length grows, misattribution becomes increasingly likely.

50
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.000
All three anti-patterns share the same root cause.

51
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
There is no clear boundary between instructions and source material.

52
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Testing attribution is the step that confirms your structure is actually working.

53
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
The most reliable method is asking the model to cite which document each claim comes from in its output.

54
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.000
If it can't, the structure isn't clear enough.

55
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Another strong test is deliberately using documents with conflicting information.

56
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.793
A well-structured prompt should let the model correctly attribute which document says what, even

57
00:04:41.793 --> 00:04:42.939
when they disagree.

58
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Using structured output formats like JSON makes attribution explicit and machine-checkable.

59
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.349
Attribution testing doesn't need to be elaborate, but it should happen before a multi-document

60
00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:55.943
prompt goes into production,

61
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
because structure problems that pass a quick read often fail under real inputs.

62
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.000
XML document structure keeps multi-part prompts reliable and debuggable.

63
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:16.000
The document, document context, and source tag pattern give each source a clean, labeled home,

64
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.000
and explicit references in your instructions connect that structure back to the task.

65
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Inline prose mixing and untagged blocks are the primary cause of misattribution,

66
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.000
so test with deliberately conflicting documents before anything goes into production.

67
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.000
That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

